What:		/sys/kernel/debug/vas/stats
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	read only
		Counters for the VAS fault path on PowerNV, one per line as
		"<name> <count>", for all VAS instances together. They are
		kept whether or not debugfs is mounted, and they only rise.
		Documentation/arch/powerpc/vas.rst describes the fault path.

		fault_crbs
			CRBs taken from a fault window's FIFO.
		fault_bad_pswid
			Fault CRBs whose send window could not be found.
		fixup
			Fault CRBs for which resolution was attempted.
		fixup_not_user_ea
			Faulting addresses that are not a user address mapped
			by the window's address space. Nothing is resolved.
		fixup_mm_gone
			Fault CRBs whose address space had already been torn
			down. Nothing is resolved.
		fixup_pages
			Pages made resident on a window's behalf.
		fixup_page_err
			Pages the page fault handler could not make resident.
			The run stops there.
		fixup_hash_err
			Pages the hash page table refused to accept (hash MMU
			only). The page stays untranslatable by the nest MMU.
		fixup_hash_noinsert
			Pages for which the hash walk found nothing to insert:
			no present page table entry, or one that does not
			permit the access (hash MMU only). This is the
			ordinary outcome while another thread migrates the
			page or a NUMA hinting scan holds it PROT_NONE; the
			page stays untranslatable and the accelerator asks for
			it again. It indicates a kernel fault only if it rises
			while the retry never succeeds.
		fixup_ste_err
			Pages whose segment could not be given a segment
			table entry (hash MMU only).
		fixup_budget
			Runs cut short by fault_page_budget. Not an error: the
			retried request faults again where the run stopped.
		fixup_refused_load, fixup_refused_store
			Accesses the nest MMU refused, because of the page's
			protection or the window's key mask, where the mapping
			confirmed the refusal. Nothing is faulted in; the
			request completes with CSB_CC_PROTECTION (load) or
			CSB_CC_WR_PROTECTION (store).
		fixup_refused_domain
			Accesses outside the domains of a window opened with
			VAS_TX_WIN_FLAG_DOMAINS. The segment is absent by
			policy, so nothing is faulted in and the request
			completes with the protection code for its direction.
		fixup_stamp_disagree
			Fault stamps that reported a refusal the mapping did
			not confirm. The address was walked as a translation
			fault instead.
		fixup_stamp_unknown
			Fault stamps with a status the kernel does not
			recognize. Treated as a translation fault.
		fixup_dir_disagree
			Faults whose stamp direction (load or store) differs
			from the direction derived from the CRB's descriptors.
		csb
			Status block updates attempted.
		csb_task_gone
			Updates dropped because neither the opening thread
			nor its thread group leader exists any more, or the
			one that does is exiting.
		csb_mm_replaced
			Updates dropped because the process no longer runs in
			the address space that issued the request: it called
			execve(), or it exited. The results of the requests
			in flight at that point are lost.
		csb_pkey_denied
			Updates not written because the key on the page
			holding the status block denies stores under the
			window's key mask.
		csb_copy_fail
			Updates whose copy to the status block address failed.
		csb_signal
			SIGSEGV (SEGV_MAPERR) signals sent for a status block
			that could not be written.
		csb_pkey_signal
			SIGSEGV (SEGV_PKUERR) signals sent for a status block
			whose page key denies the write.
		win_retained
			Windows kept after a close that did not complete. See
			/sys/kernel/debug/vas/v<N>/retained.

What:		/sys/kernel/debug/vas/fault_page_budget
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	read/write
		The most pages one fault CRB may make resident before the
		fault path moves on to the next CRB, as a decimal unsigned
		integer. The default covers 1 MiB: 256 with 4K pages, 16
		with 64K pages. A value of 0 is treated as 1. A run cut short
		by the budget is counted in fixup_budget; the retried request
		faults again where the run stopped. Raising the budget trades
		a longer stall for the other windows on the chip against
		fewer round trips for a large buffer.

What:		/sys/kernel/debug/vas/v<N>/retained
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	read only
		The number of windows on VAS instance <N> whose close did
		not complete and which the kernel keeps, with their window
		id, credits, cgroup charge, address space and hardware PID,
		until reboot. Only ever rises. A value that keeps growing
		means windows are failing to close.

What:		/sys/kernel/debug/vas/v<N>/w<id>/info
Date:		November 2017
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	read only
		The kernel's view of window <id> on VAS instance <N>: its
		coprocessor type, whether it is a send or a receive window,
		and the pid of the process that opened it.

What:		/sys/kernel/debug/vas/v<N>/w<id>/hvwc
Date:		November 2017
Contact:	linuxppc-dev@lists.ozlabs.org
Description:	read only
		The hypervisor window context registers of window <id> on
		VAS instance <N>, one per line as "0x<value> <NAME>". AMR is
		the key mask the window translates under.
